      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure I follow this\n1. the test calls this with val \u003d\u003d BIT(2)\n2. val_interrupt \u003d BIT(2) \u0026 0x0c \u003d\u003e BIT(2)\n3. control_4_cond \u003d BIT(2)\n4. reg[ctrl_4] \u003d (reg[ctrl_4] \u0026 ~ 0x0c) // clear BIT(2) and BIT(3)\n       | BIT(2); // Add BIT(2) back in because that\u0027s what val was\n       \nWhen you try to read the register below you:\n1. Read the register and store it into buf\n2. Update the register with\n   (*buf \u0026 ~SYV682X_CONTROL_4_INT_MASK) | data-\u003econtrol_4_cond\n   This removes BIT(2) and BIT(3) from the register value then adds\n   the old values back in (which is BIT(2) in this case).\n   \nSo where I\u0027m confused is that the comment says that reading the register should clear the bits, shouldn\u0027t it be just (*buf \u0026 ~SYV682X_CONTROL_4_INT_MASK)?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 87,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 94,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "cc3e62be12fbd5142c8efa53ef9847b6db005899",
